{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/gitseq-mcp at exact clean head 7e5416591aed9b9ce31db7f04b0a894bde7708ff, succeeding 8ab7abbe. This artifact exists because the merge preflight refused my merge and was right to: the head changes cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go, a maintained path I had not covered. Companion to 806c884d at cmd/gs, 780daff3 at internal/statusview, 43e1f8a8 at docs and the internal/service artifact filed alongside this one; all five must be co-signed together.\n\nWHAT CHANGED HERE: one test file, nothing else. No adapter source moves. TestSelectiveToolsUseResidentSelectionWithoutFetchingStatus gains the guard the requester's ruling 56565ba9 demanded — it sends an MCP-shaped artifacts call carrying state and then reaches, both undeclared in the tool schema, and requires the response to be identical to the declared paths-only contract. If either selector is ever honoured again, retired selects zero rows where the contract says one, and the test names the selector that changed the semantics.\n\nWHY A TEST FILE IS THE RIGHT PLACE FOR THIS AND WHY IT MATTERS AT THIS PATH. The defect the ruling closed was a schema that lied: the adapter declared paths, limit and cursor while decoding into a struct that silently honoured two more. Nothing at this path changed to fix that — the fix is the type split in internal/statusview. What this path gains is the alarm, sited at the boundary where the lie would appear. That is why the guard lives in the MCP tests rather than beside the split: a reader of statusview can see the types are separate, but only a call through the adapter can show that an undeclared field does nothing.\n\nI VERIFIED THE GUARD BITES, AND I HAD TO GET THE MUTATION RIGHT TO DO IT. Re-adding State and Reaches to ArtifactQuery leaves this test green, because the fields are dead until something reads them; I ran that mutation twice and briefly mistook a correct test for a hollow one. Making BuildArtifactPage honour them fails this test immediately with \"unde